-- AWS Certified Solutions Architect - Associate (SAA-C03).
-- Domains: 1 Design Secure Architectures (30%), 2 Design Resilient Architectures (26%),
-- 3 Design High-Performing Architectures (24%), 4 Design Cost-Optimized Architectures (20%).
insert into public.flashcards (id, cert, deck, domain, objective, front, back) values
-- Domain 1: Secure Architectures
('sa-f-001','awssaa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is the AWS shared responsibility model?$$,$$AWS secures the cloud (hardware, global infrastructure, managed service internals); the customer secures what is IN the cloud (data, IAM, OS patching for EC2, security groups, encryption config).$$),
('sa-f-002','awssaa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is an IAM role vs an IAM user?$$,$$A user is a permanent identity with long-term credentials. A role is assumed temporarily and provides short-lived credentials - preferred for EC2, Lambda, and cross-account access (no stored keys).$$),
('sa-f-003','awssaa','core',1,'1.1',$$Why attach an IAM role to an EC2 instance instead of storing access keys on it?$$,$$The role delivers automatically-rotated temporary credentials via the instance metadata service, so no long-term secrets sit on disk to be stolen.$$),
('sa-f-004','awssaa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is the principle of least privilege in IAM?$$,$$Grant only the permissions an identity needs. Start from deny, add specific allows, and use conditions - never attach broad AdministratorAccess by default.$$),
('sa-f-005','awssaa','core',1,'1.1',$$Security group vs network ACL?$$,$$Security groups are stateful, instance-level, allow-only. Network ACLs are stateless, subnet-level, and support explicit allow and deny rules. Use both as layered defense.$$),
('sa-f-006','awssaa','core',1,'1.1',$$What does "stateful" mean for a security group?$$,$$Return traffic for an allowed inbound (or outbound) request is automatically permitted - you don''t need a matching rule in the other direction. NACLs, being stateless, require both.$$),
('sa-f-007','awssaa','core',1,'1.2',$$How do you encrypt data at rest in S3?$$,$$Server-side encryption: SSE-S3 (AWS-managed keys), SSE-KMS (KMS keys with audit/rotation control), or SSE-C (customer-provided keys). Enforce with a bucket policy or default encryption.$$),
('sa-f-008','awssaa','core',1,'1.2',$$What is AWS KMS?$$,$$Key Management Service - creates and controls encryption keys (CMKs), integrates with most AWS services for envelope encryption, and logs every key use in CloudTrail.$$),
('sa-f-009','awssaa','core',1,'1.2',$$What is envelope encryption?$$,$$Data is encrypted with a data key; the data key is itself encrypted with a KMS key. Only the encrypted data key is stored alongside the data - KMS decrypts it on demand.$$),
('sa-f-010','awssaa','core',1,'1.1',$$How should you secure the AWS account root user?$$,$$Enable MFA, don''t use it for daily tasks, create IAM admin users/roles instead, and lock away the root credentials. Root can''t be restricted by IAM policy.$$),
('sa-f-011','awssaa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is an S3 bucket policy vs an IAM policy?$$,$$A bucket policy is resource-based, attached to the bucket, and can grant cross-account access. An IAM policy is identity-based, attached to a user/role/group. Both are evaluated together.$$),
('sa-f-012','awssaa','core',1,'1.1',$$How do you keep an RDS database private?$$,$$Place it in private subnets with no public IP, control access with security groups referencing the app tier, and reach it only from within the VPC or via a bastion/VPN.$$),
('sa-f-013','awssaa','core',1,'1.2',$$What is AWS Secrets Manager used for?$$,$$Storing, retrieving, and automatically rotating secrets (DB credentials, API keys) instead of hardcoding them - with fine-grained IAM access and audit logging.$$),
('sa-f-014','awssaa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is a VPC endpoint and why use one?$$,$$It lets resources reach AWS services (S3, DynamoDB via Gateway endpoints; others via Interface/PrivateLink) privately without traversing the internet - improving security and reducing NAT cost.$$),
('sa-f-015','awssaa','core',1,'1.1',$$What does AWS WAF protect?$$,$$Web applications behind CloudFront, ALB, or API Gateway - filtering common exploits (SQLi, XSS), bad bots, and rate-based floods at Layer 7.$$),
('sa-f-016','awssaa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is AWS Shield?$$,$$Managed DDoS protection. Standard is free and automatic at the network/transport layer; Advanced adds Layer 7 protections, cost protection, and DDoS response team access.$$),
('sa-f-017','awssaa','core',1,'1.2',$$How do you enforce encryption in transit to an S3 bucket?$$,$$A bucket policy that denies requests where aws:SecureTransport is false - blocking any non-HTTPS access.$$),
('sa-f-018','awssaa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is AWS Organizations SCP (Service Control Policy)?$$,$$Guardrails applied to accounts/OUs that set the maximum permissions available - they can''t grant access, only limit what member accounts can do.$$),
('sa-f-019','awssaa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is Amazon Cognito used for?$$,$$Managing authentication for web/mobile apps: user pools (sign-up/sign-in directory) and identity pools (federated temporary AWS credentials for authenticated users).$$),
('sa-f-020','awssaa','core',1,'1.2',$$What does AWS Certificate Manager (ACM) provide?$$,$$Free public TLS certificates that auto-renew, integrated with ELB, CloudFront, and API Gateway to terminate HTTPS without manual certificate management.$$),
('sa-f-021','awssaa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is cross-account access using roles?$$,$$Instead of sharing credentials, Account B''s role trusts Account A; A''s principals assume the role via STS to get temporary credentials - auditable and revocable.$$),
('sa-f-022','awssaa','core',1,'1.1',$$What is the difference between authentication and authorization in IAM?$$,$$Authentication verifies who you are (credentials, MFA). Authorization determines what you may do (policy evaluation). IAM policies handle authorization.$$),
-- Domain 2: Resilient Architectures
('sa-f-023','awssaa','core',2,'2.1',$$What is the difference between an Availability Zone and a Region?$$,$$A Region is a geographic area; each contains multiple isolated Availability Zones (separate data centers). Spread resources across AZs for high availability.$$),
('sa-f-024','awssaa','core',2,'2.1',$$How does a Multi-AZ RDS deployment provide resilience?$$,$$A synchronous standby replica in another AZ; on primary failure RDS automatically fails over to the standby with the same endpoint - for availability, not read scaling.$$),
('sa-f-025','awssaa','core',2,'2.1',$$Multi-AZ vs read replicas in RDS?$$,$$Multi-AZ is synchronous, for failover/availability (standby not readable). Read replicas are asynchronous, for scaling reads, and can be promoted for DR.$$),
('sa-f-026','awssaa','core',2,'2.1',$$How does an Auto Scaling Group improve resilience?$$,$$It maintains a desired instance count across AZs, replaces unhealthy instances automatically, and scales in/out on demand - self-healing capacity.$$),
('sa-f-027','awssaa','core',2,'2.1',$$What does an Elastic Load Balancer contribute to resilience?$$,$$It distributes traffic across healthy targets in multiple AZs and stops routing to failed instances (health checks), removing single points of failure.$$),
('sa-f-028','awssaa','core',2,'2.2',$$What is Amazon S3''s durability and how is it achieved?$$,$$11 nines (99.999999999%) durability by redundantly storing objects across multiple devices and AZs within a region.$$),
('sa-f-029','awssaa','core',2,'2.2',$$What is the purpose of S3 Versioning?$$,$$Keeps multiple versions of an object so accidental overwrites/deletes can be recovered - deletes add a delete marker rather than removing data.$$),
('sa-f-030','awssaa','core',2,'2.2',$$What is S3 Cross-Region Replication used for?$$,$$Automatically copying objects to a bucket in another region for DR, lower-latency access, or compliance. Requires versioning on both buckets.$$),
('sa-f-031','awssaa','core',2,'2.1',$$How does Route 53 support high availability?$$,$$DNS health checks with failover, latency, weighted, and geolocation routing - it can route away from an unhealthy region/endpoint automatically.$$),
('sa-f-032','awssaa','core',2,'2.2',$$What is the difference between RTO and RPO in DR planning?$$,$$RTO: how quickly you must restore service. RPO: how much data loss (in time) is tolerable. They determine which DR strategy and cost are justified.$$),
('sa-f-033','awssaa','core',2,'2.2',$$Name the four DR strategies from cheapest/slowest to most expensive/fastest.$$,$$Backup & Restore, Pilot Light, Warm Standby, Multi-Site Active/Active. Faster recovery (lower RTO/RPO) costs more to run.$$),
('sa-f-034','awssaa','core',2,'2.1',$$What is a decoupled architecture and why is it resilient?$$,$$Components communicate through queues (SQS) or topics (SNS) instead of directly, so one component''s failure or slowdown doesn''t cascade - buffering absorbs spikes.$$),
('sa-f-035','awssaa','core',2,'2.1',$$How does Amazon SQS improve resilience?$$,$$It buffers messages between producers and consumers; if consumers fail or lag, messages persist in the queue and are processed when capacity returns.$$),
('sa-f-036','awssaa','core',2,'2.2',$$What is DynamoDB Global Tables?$$,$$A multi-region, active-active replicated table providing low-latency local reads/writes and regional failover for globally distributed applications.$$),
('sa-f-037','awssaa','core',2,'2.1',$$What is the benefit of stateless application tiers?$$,$$Any instance can serve any request, so instances can be added/removed/replaced freely (Auto Scaling, failover) - store session state in DynamoDB/ElastiCache instead.$$),
('sa-f-038','awssaa','core',2,'2.2',$$How do you back up EBS volumes durably?$$,$$EBS snapshots are stored in S3 (across AZs), are incremental, and can be automated with Amazon Data Lifecycle Manager or AWS Backup.$$),
-- Domain 3: High-Performing Architectures
('sa-f-039','awssaa','core',3,'3.1',$$What is Amazon CloudFront?$$,$$A global CDN that caches content at edge locations near users, reducing latency and offloading origin servers - also integrates WAF and TLS.$$),
('sa-f-040','awssaa','core',3,'3.1',$$What is Amazon ElastiCache used for?$$,$$In-memory caching (Redis or Memcached) to offload databases and serve microsecond reads for sessions, leaderboards, and frequently accessed data.$$),
('sa-f-041','awssaa','core',3,'3.2',$$When would you choose DynamoDB over RDS?$$,$$For massive scale, single-digit-millisecond latency, key-value/document access patterns, and serverless operation - versus RDS for relational joins and complex queries.$$),
('sa-f-042','awssaa','core',3,'3.1',$$What is Amazon S3 Transfer Acceleration?$$,$$Uses CloudFront edge locations to speed long-distance uploads to S3 over the optimized AWS backbone instead of the public internet.$$),
('sa-f-043','awssaa','core',3,'3.2',$$What is the benefit of read replicas for performance?$$,$$They offload read-heavy traffic from the primary database, scaling read throughput horizontally (asynchronous replication).$$),
('sa-f-044','awssaa','core',3,'3.1',$$What are EC2 placement groups?$$,$$Cluster (low-latency, high-throughput in one AZ), Spread (distinct hardware for resilience), and Partition (large distributed workloads) - control instance placement for performance/HA.$$),
('sa-f-045','awssaa','core',3,'3.2',$$What is Amazon Kinesis used for?$$,$$Real-time ingestion and processing of streaming data (logs, clickstreams, telemetry) at scale, with ordered, replayable records.$$),
('sa-f-046','awssaa','core',3,'3.1',$$How does Auto Scaling contribute to performance, not just resilience?$$,$$It adds capacity as demand rises (target tracking, step scaling) so performance stays consistent under load, then removes it to save cost when demand falls.$$),
('sa-f-047','awssaa','core',3,'3.2',$$What EBS volume type suits high-IOPS databases?$$,$$Provisioned IOPS SSD (io1/io2) for consistent low-latency high IOPS; General Purpose SSD (gp3) for a balanced default; throughput-optimized HDD (st1) for big sequential workloads.$$),
('sa-f-048','awssaa','core',3,'3.1',$$What is Amazon Aurora and its performance benefit?$$,$$A MySQL/PostgreSQL-compatible engine with a distributed, auto-scaling storage layer, up to 15 read replicas, and much higher throughput than standard RDS.$$),
('sa-f-049','awssaa','core',3,'3.2',$$What compute option removes server management entirely for event-driven workloads?$$,$$AWS Lambda - runs code in response to events with automatic scaling and per-invocation billing; no servers to provision or patch.$$),
('sa-f-050','awssaa','core',3,'3.1',$$What is the purpose of an Application Load Balancer vs a Network Load Balancer?$$,$$ALB is Layer 7 (HTTP/S, path/host routing, WAF). NLB is Layer 4 (TCP/UDP, ultra-low latency, static IP, millions of requests/sec).$$),
-- Domain 4: Cost-Optimized Architectures
('sa-f-051','awssaa','core',4,'4.1',$$What are the EC2 purchasing options and their cost trade-offs?$$,$$On-Demand (flexible, priciest), Reserved/Savings Plans (1-3yr commitment, big discount), Spot (up to 90% off, interruptible), Dedicated Hosts (compliance, most expensive).$$),
('sa-f-052','awssaa','core',4,'4.1',$$When are Spot Instances appropriate?$$,$$For fault-tolerant, flexible, interruptible workloads (batch, CI, big data) - they can be reclaimed with a 2-minute warning, so never use them for stateful single-instance needs.$$),
('sa-f-053','awssaa','core',4,'4.1',$$What are S3 storage classes by access pattern?$$,$$Standard (frequent), Standard-IA/One Zone-IA (infrequent), Intelligent-Tiering (auto-moves), Glacier Instant/Flexible/Deep Archive (rare, cheapest, retrieval latency).$$),
('sa-f-054','awssaa','core',4,'4.1',$$What does S3 Intelligent-Tiering do?$$,$$Automatically moves objects between access tiers based on usage, optimizing cost with no performance impact or retrieval fees - ideal for unpredictable access.$$),
('sa-f-055','awssaa','core',4,'4.2',$$What is an S3 Lifecycle policy?$$,$$Rules that transition objects to cheaper storage classes or expire them after a set time - e.g., logs to Glacier after 30 days, deleted after a year.$$),
('sa-f-056','awssaa','core',4,'4.1',$$What are Savings Plans vs Reserved Instances?$$,$$Savings Plans commit to a $/hour compute spend for flexibility across instance families/regions; RIs commit to specific instance attributes. Both discount vs On-Demand for a 1-3yr term.$$),
('sa-f-057','awssaa','core',4,'4.2',$$How does right-sizing reduce cost?$$,$$Matching instance/volume types and sizes to actual utilization (via CloudWatch/Compute Optimizer) avoids paying for idle over-provisioned capacity.$$),
('sa-f-058','awssaa','core',4,'4.1',$$Why can a NAT Gateway be a cost concern, and what reduces it?$$,$$It charges hourly plus per-GB processed. VPC Gateway endpoints for S3/DynamoDB let that traffic bypass the NAT Gateway entirely, cutting data-processing cost.$$),
('sa-f-059','awssaa','core',4,'4.2',$$What tool helps visualize and forecast AWS spend?$$,$$AWS Cost Explorer for analysis/forecasting and AWS Budgets for alerts when spend or usage crosses thresholds.$$),
('sa-f-060','awssaa','core',4,'4.1',$$How does serverless (Lambda, Fargate) optimize cost for spiky workloads?$$,$$You pay only for actual execution/usage with no idle capacity - versus always-on EC2 that costs money even when unused.$$),
('sa-f-061','awssaa','core',4,'4.2',$$How does Auto Scaling optimize cost?$$,$$It removes capacity when demand drops so you stop paying for unneeded instances, then adds it back on demand - matching spend to actual load.$$),
('sa-f-062','awssaa','core',4,'4.1',$$What is the cheapest way to store rarely-accessed archival data with hours-long retrieval acceptable?$$,$$S3 Glacier Deep Archive - the lowest-cost class, designed for long-term archives retrieved rarely with retrieval times measured in hours.$$),
-- acronyms
('sa-a-001','awssaa','acronym',1,'1.1',$$IAM$$,$$Identity and Access Management - controls authentication and authorization in AWS.$$),
('sa-a-002','awssaa','acronym',1,'1.1',$$VPC$$,$$Virtual Private Cloud - isolated virtual network in AWS.$$),
('sa-a-003','awssaa','acronym',1,'1.2',$$KMS$$,$$Key Management Service - managed creation and control of encryption keys.$$),
('sa-a-004','awssaa','acronym',1,'1.1',$$SCP$$,$$Service Control Policy - Organizations guardrail capping account permissions.$$),
('sa-a-005','awssaa','acronym',1,'1.2',$$ACM$$,$$AWS Certificate Manager - free auto-renewing TLS certificates.$$),
('sa-a-006','awssaa','acronym',1,'1.1',$$STS$$,$$Security Token Service - issues temporary credentials for assumed roles.$$),
('sa-a-007','awssaa','acronym',1,'1.1',$$WAF$$,$$Web Application Firewall - Layer 7 filtering for web apps.$$),
('sa-a-008','awssaa','acronym',2,'2.1',$$AZ$$,$$Availability Zone - isolated data center location within a Region.$$),
('sa-a-009','awssaa','acronym',2,'2.1',$$ASG$$,$$Auto Scaling Group - maintains and scales a fleet of EC2 instances.$$),
('sa-a-010','awssaa','acronym',2,'2.1',$$ELB$$,$$Elastic Load Balancer - distributes traffic across healthy targets.$$),
('sa-a-011','awssaa','acronym',2,'2.1',$$SQS$$,$$Simple Queue Service - managed message queue for decoupling.$$),
('sa-a-012','awssaa','acronym',2,'2.1',$$SNS$$,$$Simple Notification Service - pub/sub messaging and fan-out.$$),
('sa-a-013','awssaa','acronym',2,'2.2',$$RTO$$,$$Recovery Time Objective - target time to restore service after an outage.$$),
('sa-a-014','awssaa','acronym',2,'2.2',$$RPO$$,$$Recovery Point Objective - tolerable data loss measured in time.$$),
('sa-a-015','awssaa','acronym',3,'3.1',$$CDN$$,$$Content Delivery Network - CloudFront edge caching for low latency.$$),
('sa-a-016','awssaa','acronym',3,'3.2',$$IOPS$$,$$Input/Output Operations Per Second - storage performance measure.$$),
('sa-a-017','awssaa','acronym',3,'3.1',$$ALB$$,$$Application Load Balancer - Layer 7 HTTP/S load balancing.$$),
('sa-a-018','awssaa','acronym',3,'3.1',$$NLB$$,$$Network Load Balancer - Layer 4 ultra-low-latency load balancing.$$),
('sa-a-019','awssaa','acronym',4,'4.1',$$RI$$,$$Reserved Instance - discounted capacity for a 1-3 year commitment.$$),
('sa-a-020','awssaa','acronym',4,'4.1',$$IA$$,$$Infrequent Access - S3 storage class for rarely accessed data.$$),
-- feynman
('sa-y-001','awssaa','feynman',1,'1.1',$$Explain the AWS shared responsibility model with a concrete EC2 example.$$,$$Model answer: AWS secures the cloud itself - the data centers, physical hosts, hypervisor, and network backbone. You secure what you put in it. For an EC2 instance that means: you patch the guest OS, configure the security group, manage IAM roles and keys, and encrypt your data. AWS guarantees the hardware and facility are secure; a misconfigured security group or unpatched OS is your responsibility, not theirs.$$),
('sa-y-002','awssaa','feynman',2,'2.1',$$Explain why spreading instances across Availability Zones matters more than adding more instances in one AZ.$$,$$Model answer: An Availability Zone is a distinct data center with its own power and networking. Ten instances in one AZ all die if that AZ has an outage - you have capacity, not resilience. Two instances split across two AZs survive the loss of either. AZs are engineered to fail independently, so multi-AZ is how you actually eliminate the single point of failure; scaling within one AZ only handles load, not failure.$$),
('sa-y-003','awssaa','feynman',2,'2.2',$$Explain how RTO and RPO drive the choice of disaster recovery strategy.$$,$$Model answer: RTO is how fast you must be back up; RPO is how much data you can afford to lose. Loose targets (hours/a day) justify cheap Backup & Restore - you rebuild from backups when needed. Tight targets (seconds/minutes) demand Warm Standby or Multi-Site active/active, which run duplicate infrastructure constantly and cost far more. You pick the cheapest strategy that still meets both numbers, because faster recovery always costs more to keep running.$$),
('sa-y-004','awssaa','feynman',1,'1.1',$$Explain why assigning an IAM role to EC2 is safer than storing access keys on the instance.$$,$$Model answer: Hardcoded access keys are long-lived secrets sitting on disk - if the instance is compromised or an image is shared, they leak and stay valid until someone notices and rotates them. An IAM role instead hands the instance short-lived temporary credentials through the metadata service, and AWS rotates them automatically. There's no permanent secret to steal, and you can change the role's permissions centrally without touching the instance.$$),
('sa-y-005','awssaa','feynman',4,'4.1',$$Explain when Spot Instances save money and when they'd be a costly mistake.$$,$$Model answer: Spot uses AWS's spare capacity at up to 90% off, but AWS can reclaim it with a two-minute warning. That's perfect for work that can pause and resume - batch processing, CI builds, big-data jobs spread across many instances. It's a mistake for anything stateful or that must stay up: a single database or a checkout service on Spot will eventually get interrupted mid-request. The rule is: Spot for interruptible and horizontally scaled, On-Demand or Reserved for must-stay-running.$$),
('sa-y-006','awssaa','feynman',3,'3.1',$$Explain how CloudFront and ElastiCache improve performance in different ways.$$,$$Model answer: Both cache, but at different layers. CloudFront is a CDN - it stores copies of your content at edge locations physically near users, so a request in Tokyo is served from Tokyo instead of crossing the ocean to your origin. That cuts latency and offloads your servers. ElastiCache sits in your backend, holding hot data (sessions, query results) in memory so your app reads it in microseconds instead of hitting the database every time. CloudFront optimizes the distance to the user; ElastiCache optimizes the load on your database.$$);
